Guess the Object

This is my project for the Artificial Intelligence and the Web seminar. I made a guessing game with Python and Streamlit. The AI chooses a secret everyday object, and the player has to find out what it is.

How to play:
Click "Start game". The AI chooses an object without showing it.
Use "Compare" to ask which of two objects is more similar to the secret object. For example: guitar or fridge?
Type a guess into the chat. The app checks the answer and shows how close it is.
If I get stuck, I can ask for a hint. There are up to eight hints per game.
The round ends when the object is guessed correctly or when the player clicks "Quit". After that, a new round can be started.
For wrong guesses, the AI also makes a short joke in a popup. If the similarity score is 0, the popup shows a cat meme. Quitting shows a different meme, an AI-generated joke, and the secret word.

How the game works:
The AI is used to choose an object, compare objects, create hints, rate guesses, and write jokes. I use the same ask_ai() function for these requests so I do not repeat the API code everywhere.
I save the current game in st.session_state. This is important because Streamlit runs the Python script again when the player clicks a button. Session state also lets the Stats page read results from the Play page.

The similarity scores mean:
0 = unrelated
1 = distant
2 = somewhat related
3 = related
4 = very close
5 = correct (also possible for another name for the same object)
These scores are estimates made by the AI. They are not always perfect. Exact matches are checked in Python first.

Stats page:
The Stats page shows the number of finished games, wins, average guesses, and average comparisons. It also has a bar chart for guesses per game and a chart showing guess quality. More detailed guesses can be opened separately.
The statistics are only stored for the current Streamlit session. I am not using a database, so they are not saved permanently.

Project files:
- main.py starts the app and connects the Play and Stats pages.
- guessing_game.py contains the game logic, AI prompts, chat, hints, and popups.
- statistics.py calculates and displays the game statistics.
- requirements.txt lists the Python libraries the app needs.
- cat.png is the cat logo used on the Play page.
- are-you-serious-meme.png and quitter.png are the two popup images.

If you want to run it locally:
python -m streamlit run main.py

All cat images are AI generated ^-^ .